---
// Real data now: classic.com's own real market chart (median / 25th-75th percentile / range by
// model year, 1976-1994, public/data/toyota-truck/market_classiccom_years.json), a one-time
// hand-read of that real chart rather than a live scrape (classic.com sits behind Cloudflare's
// bot-challenge for unauthenticated automated access, and the chart's own data loads over a live
// connection after the page renders, not in the static HTML, so this isn't something a repeatable
// collector script could pull, see src/lib/toyota-truck/market-value.ts). Alongside that: Hagerty's
// own #3-Good condition guide values and 18 individually-traceable real sales pulled from Hagerty's
// past-sales aggregation (itself sourced from Bring a Trailer, Cars & Bids, and Collecting Cars)
// combined with a few sales verified directly. All built by
// scripts/toyota-truck/build-market-value-data.ts from the raw collected/hand-captured CSVs.
// A widely-repeated $60,500 1985 4Runner figure found in an early pass couldn't be traced back to
// an actual sale anywhere and was left out rather than repeated on faith.
---

<section id="market-value" class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-16 md:px-12 md:py-20">
	<h2 class="font-anton text-4xl md:text-5xl">What the Toyota truck market looks like</h2>
	<p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		Real market data from classic.com covering nearly this truck's entire production run
		(1976-1994), 18 individually-traceable real sales I traced by hand, and Hagerty's own
		published guide values layered on top. Not a full census, but enough to see the real spread,
		and how far individual sales can land from the broader market.
	</p>

	<div id="mv-value-summary" class="mt-6 raw-box rounded-xl bg-cream p-4 font-mono text-sm"></div>
	<div id="mv-value-chart" class="mt-4"></div>
	<script src="../../../scripts/the-22re-restoration/market-value-chart.ts"></script>

	<div class="mt-8 max-w-[65ch] space-y-4">
		<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			Condition and paperwork move the price more than the calendar does, and the data actually
			backs that up: across the 14 sales with a real mileage figure attached, mileage and price
			barely correlate at all (r = 0.16). A 1984 4Runner with 333,000 miles sold for nearly double
			a 1984 4Runner with 139,800 miles, mileage alone doesn't explain that, documented history and
			condition do. An accident-disclosed, heavily modified truck and a documented, low-mileage one
			from the same era can still be worlds apart, mileage just isn't the variable doing the work.
			A purpose-built replica, like the McFly tribute build I mentioned earlier, sits in its own
			category entirely. It's valuable for what it recreates, not for being a clean example of the
			base truck.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h3 class="font-display mt-14 text-lg font-semibold">By model year, next to what it cost new</h3>
	<p class="mt-2 max-w-[65ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		A 1984 Toyota Pickup started at $6,188 new, up to $10,338 loaded (SR5, 4WD, extended cab).
		Below that: classic.com's own real market data, median price plus the 25th-75th percentile
		band plus the full range, for every model year from 1976 to 1994, real coverage of almost this
		truck's entire production run. The smaller blue dots are this page's own individually-traced
		sales laid on top as a cross-check, two independent sources landing in the same neighborhood.
	</p>
	<div id="mv-model-year-chart" class="mt-4"></div>
	<div id="mv-model-year-legend" class="mt-2 text-xs text-ink-soft"></div>
	<script src="../../../scripts/the-22re-restoration/market-value-by-model-year-chart.ts"></script>

	<div class="mt-6 max-w-[65ch] space-y-4">
		<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			The real top end: a 1991 Toyota Pickup 4x4, genuine 22R-E, 69,981 miles, listed by Vanguard
			Motor Sales (a real Michigan dealership) at $69,900, close to a dollar a mile and more than a
			new Tacoma TRD Pro. The Drive covered it and put a real number on how rare that is: "only two
			examples of this generation of Toyota Pickup...have crested $50,000 at auction."
		</p>
	</div>

	<h3 class="font-display mt-14 text-lg font-semibold">What it costs to just own one</h3>
	<p class="mt-2 max-w-[65ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		The $70k trucks are the outliers. A Jalopnik writer bought a rust-free 1988 Toyota Pickup for
		$3,100, put $1,190.82 into real repairs (the only itemized one: a $305 catalytic converter)
		plus $543.83 in registration, tax, and insurance, and sold it eight months and roughly 90 labor
		hours later for $8,000, a documented $3,165.35 profit. Worth being upfront: that particular
		truck had the V6, not the 22R-E this page is about, but it's real, honest color for what
		actually owning and working on one of these costs day to day, not just what the rare ones
		fetch at auction.
	</p>

	<h3 class="font-display mt-14 text-lg font-semibold">By year sold, and an honest limit on what this shows</h3>
	<p class="mt-2 max-w-[65ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		I wanted this to show a real price-over-time trend as these trucks get rarer. It mostly can't,
		yet: 13 of the 16 dated sales here happened in the last few months of 2026, because Hagerty's
		free tier only surfaces its most recent past sales, the other roughly 278 in its own archive
		are paywalled behind a paid membership, and Bring a Trailer's own results archive isn't
		something I could pull from directly either. What's below is the real, honest scatter, not a
		smoothed trend line I don't have the data to support.
	</p>
	<div id="mv-calendar-year-chart" class="mt-4"></div>
	<script src="../../../scripts/the-22re-restoration/market-value-by-calendar-year-chart.ts"></script>

	<h3 class="font-display mt-14 text-lg font-semibold">Are these becoming a sought-after classic?</h3>
	<p class="mt-2 max-w-[65ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		A different, real signal for that: Google search interest, 2004-2026, for two repair/maintenance
		searches, "22re" and "22r" (someone with one of these trucks already), against two
		collector-framing searches, "vintage toyota pickup" and "90s toyota truck" (someone shopping for
		one as a classic). The pattern holds across both pairs, not just one term I happened to pick:
		"22re" and "22r" climbed together for a decade, both peaked in 2016, and have been falling
		together since, back to roughly where they started twenty years ago. "Vintage toyota pickup" and
		"90s toyota truck" both barely registered before 2020, stayed low through 2025, then both jumped
		in 2026 so far, a real pattern, though 2026 is only 8 months in and the absolute numbers here are
		small enough to be genuinely noisy. One honest methodology note:
		Google changed how Trends collects data on January 1, 2016, so the earlier and later halves of
		this chart aren't a perfectly clean apples-to-apples comparison, the post-2016 decline in the
		repair searches and the recent uptick in the collector-framing ones are the parts worth trusting,
		not the exact shape of the whole 22-year line. This data was pulled once, by hand, from Google's
		own Trends tool rather than scraped automatically, since Google's robots.txt asks crawlers not to
		hit that page.
	</p>
	<div id="mv-search-interest-chart" class="mt-4"></div>
	<script src="../../../scripts/the-22re-restoration/search-interest-chart.ts"></script>
</section>
